# This file starts by ignoring everything, then names what is public. A denylist
# fails open — anything added later ships unless somebody remembers to exclude
# it — and git history cannot be unpublished. An allowlist fails closed instead:
# a new top-level path stays invisible until it is named here.
#
# **It was a denylist until 2026-08-19**, which was safe only because the
# repository had no remote. It has one now, so the failure mode changed from
# "an untracked file sits in `git status`" to "an untracked file is on the
# internet", and those are not the same risk.
#
# Order matters: ignore all, allow the public paths, then re-ignore build output
# and oversized specimens that live inside them.
#
# **A new file at the repository root is invisible until it is named below**, and
# it fails silently: `git status` will not mention it, `git add` will refuse it
# with no message worth reading, and nothing else goes wrong. That is the
# allowlist working as designed, and it is also the only way it bites.
# `git check-ignore -v <path>` says which line is responsible.
#
# **`notes/` is deliberately absent and must stay absent.** It is where working
# notes go, and the whole point of this design is that it takes an edit to this
# file to publish one.

/*

# The core, and the command line over it. `fathom-core` owns the walk, the fold,
# the classifier and the pricing; `fathom-cli` is the single entry point both
# packages invoke as a subprocess.
!/fathom-core/
!/fathom-cli/

# The two bindings, laid out the way the siblings lay theirs out — the
# r-universe registry addresses a package by `subdir`, and `r-pkg/<name>` is the
# shape it expects. Neither package has a dependency, and each is a thin wrapper
# that finds the binary and hands back its bytes.
!/r-pkg/
!/py-pkg/

# The corpus is the instrument. Real documents only, graded the same way, with
# provenance and a date — a claim nobody can re-run is not evidence.
!/corpus/

# The design record: the ORACLE `probe.py` is diffed against, the instruments
# that read it, and the decisions with the day they were made.
!/design/

# The generated cases for the health verb, and the five scorers. NOT the corpus.
!/test/

# The manual. It stays with the core because every report in it is computed by
# running the frozen probe against the real corpus at render time, so one change
# to either invalidates every page at once.
!/book/

# The hex sticker, drawn as geometry.
!/images/

!/.github/

# Root files. `QUESTIONS.md` is the method's specification and the book reprints
# its list; the working record — the agreement, the findings log and the session
# handoffs — lives in `notes/`, which is a repository of its own and which the
# `/*` above hides from this one. `README.md` says so, so the citations to those
# documents scattered through this tree are deliberate rather than broken.
#
# `/CLAUDE.md` is deliberately NOT named here. It is a symlink to
# `notes/CLAUDE.md`, so that Claude Code — which loads a working agreement only
# from the project root — still finds it while the file itself stays private.
!/README.md
!/QUESTIONS.md
!/LICENSE
!/NOTICE
!/Cargo.toml
!/Cargo.lock
!/pyproject.toml
!/uv.lock
# **The interpreter, pinned — and `uv.lock` does NOT do this.** The lock pins
# the packages; `requires-python = ">=3.14"` is a floor, so uv takes whatever
# Python the machine offers above it. The health verb reports the PARSER'S OWN
# error text, and CPython rewords those between versions, so an unpinned
# interpreter makes `parity.py` fail on a machine that is not this laptop —
# which is exactly what CI found on its first run, on two truncated `\u` cases.
!/.python-version
!/.gitattributes
!/.gitignore

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Re-ignored, inside the paths allowed above
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Attempts leave junk behind; the corpus and the findings are the artifact.
.Rhistory
.RData
__pycache__/
*.pyc
.venv/
.DS_Store

# A source file over about 5 MB gets a fetch.sh instead of being committed.
# Anything matching this is a mistake rather than a policy.
corpus/**/*.large.json

# 14-nyc-311's source is 28 MB, so `corpus/14-nyc-311/fetch.sh` is what is
# committed and the download sits here untracked. Named individually rather than
# by a `corpus/**/source.json` rule, because FIFTEEN entries do commit theirs and
# a blanket ignore would read as though the corpus keeps none of them. It was
# showing in `git status` indefinitely, which is an invitation to the `git add`
# the size rule exists to prevent.
corpus/14-nyc-311/source.json
# 20-homebrew-formulae's source is 29.6 MB, same rule, same reason.
corpus/20-homebrew-formulae/source.json
# 21-crossref-works is 7.5 MB. The prediction said 2-5 MB and it was wrong,
# which is the only reason this line exists rather than a committed source.
corpus/21-crossref-works/source.json
# 25-usgs-quakes is 7.4 MB. Its prediction said 8-25 MB and was wrong the other
# way, which changed nothing: either side of 5 MB is what decides this list.
corpus/25-usgs-quakes/source.json
# 26-gharchive-scale ships at 118 MB gzipped and 870 MB raw. Same rule as 04,
# 14, 20, 21 and 25 above: fetch.sh is the artifact, the download is not.
corpus/26-gharchive-scale/source.json.gz
corpus/26-gharchive-scale/source.jsonl

# The health-verb test cases are generated and deterministic. The generator is
# the artifact; the tiny files are not. `uv run test/generate.py` rebuilds them.
# The count deliberately is NOT written here — it lived here as 189 while the
# suite reported 193, which is the sixth time a number in two places went stale
# in one. VERDICT.md owns it.
test/cases/

# Rust build output. `Cargo.lock` IS committed — this workspace builds a binary
# rather than a library, and the lock is the R and Python packages' only
# equivalent of `uv.lock`. `CLAUDE.md` records that Python is exactly
# reproducible here and R is not; the core is now the third case and it is the
# reproducible kind.
target/

# The engine, copied into the R package at install time by `configure`. It is
# built output that happens to live inside a source tree.
r-pkg/fathom/inst/bin/

# The rendered book, and the caches that make a re-render fast. The `.qmd`
# sources and the theme are the artifact; `_book/` is derived from them plus the
# corpus plus the binary, and `quarto render` rebuilds it.
#
# `_freeze/` is where knitr keeps the OUTPUT of each chapter's code cells, and
# it is ignored on purpose even though committing it would make a render
# reproducible without the tools. This book computes its numbers by running the
# real probe against the real corpus precisely so a stale number is impossible;
# a committed freeze cache is exactly the stale copy that rule exists to
# prevent. Delete it to force a re-measure.
book/_book/
book/.quarto/
book/_freeze/
book/site_libs/
book/.venv/
book/**/*_files/

# Editor and tooling reports. `.VSCodeCounter` writes results.* and diff.* line
# counts, which are derived from the code, regenerate on demand, and are stale
# the moment anything changes. Committing them would put a per-file line count
# in a second place with no owner in CLAUDE.md's table — the same failure the
# block above is a monument to.
.VSCodeCounter/

# The two bindings' build artifacts. The SOURCE of each package is committed; a
# tarball, a wheel and an R CMD check directory are all rebuilt in seconds from
# it, and a committed build is a second copy of the code with nobody responsible
# for refreshing it.
fathom_*.tar.gz
*.Rcheck/
py-pkg/fathom/dist/
py-pkg/fathom/*.egg-info/
# `python -m build` leaves a staging tree here, and `setup.py` copies the ENGINE
# into it — so this directory holds a 1.2 MB binary that the allowlist would
# otherwise publish, because `!/py-pkg/` un-ignores everything beneath it. Caught
# on the first wheel build, staged and not yet committed.
py-pkg/fathom/build/
r-pkg/fathom/tools/rust/
